quick_facts: Same protons, different neutrons — same element, different mass
quick_facts: Name coined by Frederick Soddy (1913): 'same place' in Greek
quick_facts: Stable examples: carbon-12 · radioactive: carbon-14
quick_facts: Uranium-235 fissions easily; uranium-238 barely does
quick_facts: Radioisotopes power medicine, dating, and tracing

sources:
    {"organization":"Britannica","title":"Isotope","url":"https://www.britannica.com/science/isotope"}

definition:
    One of the variants of a chemical element: same number of protons, different numbers of neutrons, so the same chemistry but a different mass. Chemist Frederick Soddy coined the name in 1913 — Greek for 'same place', since isotopes occupy the same spot on the periodic table. Every element has them; some are stable forever (carbon-12), others radioactive and decaying at fixed rates (carbon-14). Which isotope you have determines everything about its nuclear behavior while changing nothing about its chemistry.

debunking_snippet:
    “'Isotope' just means radioactive.” No — every element has several isotopes, and the majority are perfectly stable. Radioactive ones are called radionuclides.
